  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nQuiet weather continues, with an overall dry atmospheric profile \nmeaning there's no moisture for the weak cold front that's stalled \nout from southwest MN into northern WI to work with to produce cloud \ncover let alone rainfall. This front will wash out overnight, with \nmultiple days of southerly flow expected through Saturday. This will \nsupport highs 15 to 20 degrees above normal this week, as our \nOctober of exceptional warmth continues. Based on recent \nperformance, continued the trend of the previous forecast of going \nwith the NBM 90th percentile for highs for today through Saturday. \nTo get record highs, we need lower 80s for highs, with mid/upper 70s \ncurrently expected by the end of the week, so warm, but not quite \nrecord high at the moment.\n\nFor the weekend, models continue to show a weak cold front moving \ninto MN. This front will be running into ridge axis from the Gulf to \nthe Great Lakes, with the parent low driving this front staying well \nnorthwest of MN. This means forcing on the front consists of shallow \nconvergence and moisture near the surface that is hopefully enough \nto create some rain showers. Timing and placement of the front \ncontinues to be varied, with NBM still keeping pops in the 30-50% \nrange, with the highest PoPs coming on Saturday. This doesn't look \nlike a big rain maker or hazardous weather producer, more of a \nnuisance to any outdoor plans you have if you happen to meet up with \nsome rain along this boundary. What does happen is high pressure \nmoves in behind this front, bringing temperatures back down to near \nnormal. It's just a question of does that cooler air show up on \nSunday or Monday.\n\nBeyond the forecast, the 8-12 day period (end of October) looks \npotentially interesting from a mean upper air and jet pattern with \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. However, over the last couple of weeks, \nthe 8-12 period has often looked interesting, but that interesting \nweather has always stayed 8-12 days out. So while we work on our \nlate season sun tans the rest of the week, it will be interesting to \nsee if we can finally bring a more active pattern to within the 8-12 \nday window.",
